WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.780
What pivotal scene did Elvis star Austin Butler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:08.820
insist on changing? And why'd he call&nbsp;
it out? Elvis' momma would be proud.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:14.940
With such projects as Strictly Ballroom and Moulin&nbsp;
Rouge under his belt, it's no great shock that Baz&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:21.120
Luhrmann decided to tackle the story of the King&nbsp;
of Rock and Roll himself, Elvis Presley. Starring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:27.360
Austin Butler as the titular music legend, Elvis&nbsp;
follows his rise to fame and eventual professional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:32.400
and personal decline. It also takes a look at the&nbsp;
relationships he shared with those in his life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:36.900
including his partner, Priscilla, and&nbsp;
his longtime manager, Colonel Tom Parker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:42.960
as well as the condition of the United States&nbsp;
and the world across the 1950s, '60s, and '70s.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:48.900
That's a lot of ground to cover in one film&nbsp;
— hence why Luhrmann has a four-hour cut that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:53.820
we'll likely never see — but simply filling out&nbsp;
a checklist of important moments and events isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:59.400
enough. This is Elvis Presley, after all, one&nbsp;
of the most famous and recognizable musicians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:05.820
ever to walk a stage or grace an album cover, so&nbsp;
getting it just right is of the utmost importance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:11.460
Thankfully, Austin Butler doesn't phone it&nbsp;
in with his take on Elvis Presley. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:15.180
the star was so dedicated to the&nbsp;
production that he even gave some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:20.220
brutally honest creative feedback to Luhrmann&nbsp;
regarding a pivotal scene he found "corny."

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:24.240
In an interview with Entertainment&nbsp;
Weekly, Baz Luhrmann and Austin Butler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:29.040
explained the process of pinning down Elvis&nbsp;
Presley's speech patterns and music vocals.

00:01:29.234 --> 00:01:34.034
"It was very daunting at first,&nbsp;
uh, but just the joy of my life."

00:01:34.034 --> 00:01:38.460
This proved no easy feat and required&nbsp;
a lot of effort on Butler's part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:44.580
but they eventually got it down to a science. In&nbsp;
doing so, they even changed a scene about Elvis'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:50.306
attempt at inspiring his Vegas band during their&nbsp;
workshopping of the song "That's Alright (Mama)."

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.600
"I uh, I want to try something new."

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:59.700
When it finally came time to shoot after&nbsp;
months of rehearsal, Luhrmann recalls,

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:09.780
Luhrmann instructed the band to play different&nbsp;
notes so Butler could find a desired sound that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:14.520
sounded more sincere and true to the role.&nbsp;
While this tweak made it possible for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:19.500
actor to slip into his role more authentically,&nbsp;
this level of commitment didn't come without a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:25.320
price. Butler told Collider in June of 2022&nbsp;
that rediscovering himself after wrapping up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:30.180
his work on "Elvis" wasn't a quick and easy&nbsp;
thing to pull off. He said that the project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:36.180
required him to practically isolate and become&nbsp;
the closest to Elvis he possibly could, adding,

00:02:45.615 --> 00:02:49.935
"It's sort of an existential crisis, you&nbsp;
know? You kind of question who you are."

00:02:49.935 --> 00:02:55.500
Austin Butler clearly gave his all for Elvis&nbsp;
and wanted to do right by the pop culture icon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.500 --> 00:03:01.440
and identifying a corny scene isn't the only way&nbsp;
Butler helped Elvis become the success that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:07.140
turned out to be. In fact, the movie may not have&nbsp;
reached the finish line at all if the actor hadn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:12.060
pushed for it when the going really got tough.&nbsp;
Viewers may recall a frightening moment from early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:19.380
2020 when Elvis star and actor Tom Hanks revealed&nbsp;
that he had contracted COVID-19. As it turns out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:23.640
Hanks' illness came right around the time&nbsp;
that Elvis was in its most crucial stage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:28.380
of production. With one of the film's lead&nbsp;
actors out of action, things quickly went sour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:32.400
and it even seemed to Baz Luhrmann&nbsp;
like the film was going down in flames.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.820
During a speech at the 2023&nbsp;
Palm Springs Film Festival,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:40.440
Luhrmann spoke about this tumultuous&nbsp;
time in Elvis" development, saying,

00:03:43.746 --> 00:03:47.760
Luhrmann recalled that he subsequently&nbsp;
went to Butler and told him the film was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:52.080
over and production had shut down.&nbsp;
However, Butler remained resolute,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:57.074
insisting that he wouldn't exit the film&nbsp;
